ions quickly, creates positive atmosphere, but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Also keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, but know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate now seems best. B's crew cooperation concerns are significant for cockpit teamwork. C's communication difficulty also a concern, but maybe less severe? C creates positive atmosphere, which suggests some teamwork. However egocentric and difficulty communicating ideas could be problematic.\n\nWe could argue that C's strengths (reliability, decision speed, calm in crisis) outweigh his communication issues, especially compared to B's uncooperative attitude. So we might shift recommendation to C.\n\nAlternatively, D has responsibility, problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all may be similar to B's issues. A has technical strengths but also show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nThus likely C is best.\n\nWe need to state our current recommendation: Candidate C. Provide reasoning: B's crew cooperation concerns are serious; C's reliability and decision quality, plus positive atmosphere, outweigh his communication difficulty. Also note C keeps calm in crisis, technical competence. So we vote for C.\n\nProceed with public message."}
